taxes  at least for me there's no more  unwelcome letter in the mailbox then  that plain white envelope with a little  window bearing the eagle the scales and  the olive branch of the Internal Revenue  Service let's wondered about the olive  branch I don't know what the olive  branch has to do with the scales I can  see because you feel like you're being  weighed when I get one I don't know if  you ever do this maybe it's just my  personality I tend to put it on the  kitchen counter and let it breathe for a  few days because I'm afraid to look at  it even if I have absolutely no reason  to believe that the news is bad still  because it says Internal Revenue Service  on it I am afraid of that envelope it's  worth talking about in church even  though the idea of talking about taxes  in church doesn't seem to make any sense  I mean what two things could be more  different than taxes and church nothing  to do with each other but what makes me  think about it is that most of us have  so few times when we worry about the  power of the state over us we just don't  think about well yes there are new haven  parking tickets but that's a whole other  thing but most of us have little reason  to fear the man in uniform knocking at  our door but somehow when that IRS  envelope arrives I can't help but  imagine how I might look in an orange  jumpsuit  taxes the unwelcome intrusion of the  state into my business my money as the  Tea Partiers say that intrusion with the  subtle implied threat of pay up or go to  jail I'm not going to talk about tax  policy today I can see yang is yawning  already tax policy nothing more boring  in the world but what I want to talk  about is this sign of power over me why  do I get afraid why not get afraid about  this envelope it's because I know these  people have absolute power over me and  it makes me think about the many parts  of my life that are actually governed by  other people and by forces that exert  tremendous influence on me but that I do  not include in my impression of myself i  do not see myself as a subject of the  Internal Revenue Service but clearly it  exerts great influence on me if I'm this  afraid of that envelope so it gets me  thinking about what else in my life is  exerting influence on me that changes me  that I don't even include in my picture  of myself perhaps my employer perhaps my  professor perhaps my crazy roommate what  else is it in my life that is changing  me without me knowing now imagine  yourself a citizen of a defeated country  subject to a ruthless and powerful  Empire where some far away Emperor  orders yet another tax on your land on  your person on your income and forced by  soldiers who seem to hate your culture  and your religion the threat of taxes  comes not in a clean white envelope in  the mail but comes with a sword Oh close  to your threat  what should a faithful person do pay  their taxes and support their own  oppression or resist and die this is the  question the Pharisees and the Herodians  come to Jesus with they say in their  most flattering tone you are an honest  man of Jesus you tell the truth no  matter what people think so tell us  should we pay taxes to caesar just like  the most inviting host on Fox News or  MSNBC gonna put you in a trap with an  unanswerable question if Jesus says yes  he's no Jewish prophet if he says no the  plot against him gains another weapon  his treason against the Empire so Jesus  doesn't answer instead he asks for the  coin used to pay the tax because it has  to be a special room and coin and he  says whose picture is on it whose image  and its opponents say Caesars the  Emperor's and Jesus says well then give  Caesar what bears his image but give God  what bears God's image you can imagine  the Pharisees as the gospel says  wandering away thinking what a weird and  possibly wise answer you know if which i  think is the way they feel about Jesus  most the time weird but possibly wise  but wait a minute he never really  answered that question never said should  we pay taxes or not Jesus won't be  trapped by flattering words he won't be  trapped by the desire to win an argument  he doesn't I think even really want to  set the Pharisees straight I think his  words are for the poor and the lost and  they are the ones who hear his message  the last three weeks of church we've  heard parables about loyalty the two  sons which will serve the father the  wedding guests will they respond to the  invitation who is truly loyal to God  how do we know and today following that  we get a practical test broad question  of loyalty how does it come down to  earth it's about taxes but it's really  about more than taxes it's about all the  ways that the world pulls us away from  God taxes themselves aren't evil they  are just one obligation one more force  acting upon us that we don't know what  to do with but taxes can seem so  unreligious so unchurched Lee that we  forget that those obligations do affect  our life of faith they have power over  us so I want you to imagine yourself as  a NASCAR driver okay you have a bright  jumpsuit and on it our patches of all  your sponsors we've ever seen a NASCAR  driver who must have a hundred patches  on him of all the companies that are  paying for him I wonder if he has them  dekalb on his teeth and things too I  don't know but I want you to imagine  what patches your jumpsuit would carry  who demands your loyalty and your time  what hey who are your sponsors who's  paying for you maybe academics maybe  success or security big patches that  takes a lot of our time and loyalty  certainly our families activities arts  and clubs and service and then there are  the things that really take our time  that we don't want to think about like  Facebook and video games and clothes and  food that really occupy more time than  we like to think and then the truly  hidden ones that really consume our  energy pointless worrying grudges  self-doubt self-pity and V of other  people  one of the best sermons I read on this  passage is by Judith Johnson see both  and she writes we want to do everything  rather than select some things to devote  ourselves to as the top priority and let  others go and we what we end up doing is  giving God the balance left over when  everything else has been accomplished  all the things were loyal to all the  labels we bear she quotes Thomas a Kelly  and my favorite one of my favorite books  called a testament of devotion he writes  we are trying to be several people at  the same time without all ourselves  being organized by a single mastering  life within us each of us tends to be  not a single self but whole committee of  cells and each of ourselves in turn is a  rank individualist not cooperating with  the other selves but shouting out there  their vote loudly for themselves when  voting time comes it is it is as if we  have a chairman of our committee of many  cells who does not integrate the many  into one but who merely counts the votes  at each decision and leave disgruntled  minorities we are not integrated we are  distraught we feel honestly the pull of  many obligations and we try to fulfill  them all but life is meant to be lived  from a center a divine center but most  of us I fear have not surrendered the  rest in order to discover the holy  within when pieces asks Pharisees whose  images on the coin the word for image is  the same word when translated into Greek  from Genesis 1 26 to 27 then God said  let us make human kind in our image  according to our likeness so God created  humankind in His image in the image of  God he created them male and female he  created them if we give to our  obligations to Caesar the coins that  bear their image what's left to give to  God only everything only our whole  selves which bear the image of God would  give that small image to Caesar what is  it that bears the image of God just all  of us and all of our lives perhaps it's  because the divine image we bear seem so  distant so instead we bear the image of  the pressures on us of the society that  made us of every demanding advertisement  we've ever watched of every obligation  we feel we need to bear perhaps it would  be easier to say no to these limitless  demands to give the fullness of our  lives to God if we realize that who we  are is not the things we imagine or fear  other people think we are who we are is  a holy people made the image of God my  fear often is that I will be a failure  unloved unrecognized unrewarded and that  fear is even more powerful than the fear  of the state even more powerful than the  fear of the IRS Jesus would have us  reverse our culture's formula that I  should live in fear of what I am NOT and  let that determine my priorities Jesus  says live in to what you are divinely  created love since the day of your birth  forgiven even the sins you can't forgive  yourself filled with god's spirit  destined for God's kingdom I am NOT an  empty vessel waiting to be filled  by life I am NOT a nameless cipher  waiting for achievement or relationship  to save me I am a child of God and on  that basis I am empowered to say no to  everything else that exerts power over  me I Know Who I am and I know whose I am  it's easy to forget in our secular  culture that there are fewer reminders  of obligation and faith than the young  men who gathered here a hundred and  fifty years ago had they had daily  required chapel and twice on Sunday  sometimes up to six hours of church  though many of them resisted and they  didn't their world was one where  Christian faith was presumed and the  world worked on a Christian basis  something look back on that world with  nostalgia but i prefer the world we have  where my Catholic or Jewish neighbors  have the same rights I do in particular  society however we have to work harder  at maintaining our priorities no time  will be set aside for us for faith it  will not be required we will not learn  the Bible in school we live in a world  where the easy presumption that  Protestant faith is normal is gone the  world we live in is I think still a  better world because when Christian  becomes normal it usually ceases to be  really Christian but I think the  temptation this world puts us in is to  make faith a hobby an unusual interest  that I happen to have like collecting  antiques or following the Chicago Cubs  something I could change maybe  something I do when nothing important is  happening the rest of the world will not  give us time for our faith we have to  fight for it the rest of the world won't  tell us what we believe we have to read  the scriptures for ourselves if we find  the time the time to remember in whose  image we were made in whose spirit we  live we can overthrow the tyranny of the  Caesars the many of them the false gods  of obligation and money and elitism and  beauty and success and power but I will  not leave you where Jesus left you  should we pay our taxes doesn't Jesus  finally care about injustice and  oppression yes he does but he knows we  pay much higher taxes to our own fears  and those thrust upon us that we do to  the Empire and only when we are free  from the taxes of desire can we truly  challenge Caesars order amen

